<!--
     Aurora's SystemUI resource overlay. The second directory under this root, and the first that
     touches a package rather than the framework.

     ADR-014 put Aurora's first APK in SystemUI's process, as a plugin behind the extension point
     VolumeDialogComponent already exposes. This file is the other half of that: without it the
     plugin is refused on any build that is not debuggable.
-->
<resources>

    <!--
         SystemUI plugins loadable on user builds.

         PluginActionManager.kt:222 and PluginInstance.kt:301:

             if (!buildInfo.isDebuggable && !config.isPrivileged(component)) { ... }

         The list behind isPrivileged comes from exactly one place - PluginsModule.java:99 reads
         this array - and "privileged" here has nothing to do with priv-app or
         PRIVATE_FLAG_PRIVILEGED. It is only membership in this string-array.

         Sprint 09 Task 2 found this by reading the gate rather than running anything, and assumed
         no available machine could exercise it - every bootable product here is userdebug, so
         isDebuggable would be true and the check would never fire.

         That assumption was measured on 2026-08-07 and is FALSE. getprop ro.debuggable returns 0 on
         the emulator, Build.java:1851 derives IS_DEBUGGABLE from exactly that property, and
         vendor/lineage/config/common.mk:42 sets it deliberately: "Set ro.debuggable=0 for
         userdebug". Lineage hardens userdebug, so this gate fires HERE, not only on some future
         user build. The entry below is required by the machine on the desk.

         A plugin refused by this gate is not crashed and not logged - just absent, with AOSP's own
         dialog continuing to work, which is why it has to be checked rather than noticed.

         AOSP ships one entry here and Lineage's own overlay adds two more, including
         org.lineageos.settings.device. Adding a vendor plugin to this array is a move the tree
         already makes.

         WARNING, and it is the reason Task 3 opens with a measurement: vendor/lineage's overlay
         defines this same array, and Aurora's overlay root is appended to PRODUCT_PACKAGE_OVERLAYS
         after Lineage's. Which one survives into the built SystemUI has not been measured. If
         Lineage's wins, this file does nothing and says nothing about it.

         The first three entries are not decoration. An overlay REPLACES a resource; it does not
         merge into it. A file here listing only Aurora would win the race and take the wallet
         plugin and org.lineageos.settings.device down with it - which is the failure mode of
         winning, and it is quieter than the failure mode of losing. Both directions of this
         measurement have to be read.
    -->
    <string-array name="config_pluginAllowlist" translatable="false">
        <!-- AOSP's entry. -->
        <item>com.android.systemui</item>
        <!-- Lineage's two, carried because an overlay replaces rather than merges. -->
        <item>com.android.systemui.plugin.globalactions.wallet</item>
        <item>org.lineageos.settings.device</item>
        <!-- Aurora's, and the only line this file exists to add. ADR-014. -->
        <item>aurora.platform.systemui</item>
    </string-array>

</resources>
